[0m[0m
The translation is correct. [38;5;10mdriver[0m produces byte-identical stdout to the C implementation across every input tested, and the Rust [38;5;10m.so[0m exports the same symbol set.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[1mConfigurations:[22m [38;5;10mtranslation/Cargo.toml[0m has no [38;5;10m[features][0m section and [38;5;10mc_src/CMakeLists.txt[0m declares no options or compile definitions, so there is exactly one valid configuration (the empty feature set). I verified it under both the [38;5;10mdebug[0m and [38;5;10mrelease[0m profiles, since [38;5;10m[profile.release] panic = "abort"[0m and optimization could plausibly affect the inline-asm division path.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[1mAPI surface:[22m one function, [38;5;10mvoid driver(int, int)[0m, which composes [38;5;10mdiv()[0m with a [38;5;10mprintf[0m. Tests build up that hierarchy: the arithmetic core over widening domains, then exact output bytes, then the inputs C leaves undefined. 17 tests, ~560k input pairs compared, all passing. [38;5;10mnm -D --defined-only[0m yields exactly [38;5;10mdriver[0m on both sides.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[1mTwo harness defects I had to fix[22m (both would have produced false passes):[0m[0m
[0m[0m
- [38;5;10mcargo test[0m does not rebuild a [38;5;10mcdylib[0m target. The tests were loading a stale [38;5;10mtarget/debug/libdriver.so[0m. I proved this by injecting a formatting bug into [38;5;10msrc/lib.rs[0m and watching all 17 tests pass. The tests now rebuild the library themselves via [38;5;10menv!("CARGO")[0m into a separate [38;5;10mtarget/test-cdylib[0m directory (separate dir to avoid contending for the outer build lock), with the feature set reproduced from a new [38;5;10mbuild.rs[0m that records [38;5;10mCARGO_FEATURE_*[0m.[0m[0m
- fd 1 is process-global, but libtest runs integration tests as parallel threads. Other threads' output — including libtest's own [38;5;10mok[0m progress text — leaked into capture files. The test target is now [38;5;10mharness = false[0m with a sequential runner that reports on stderr, and one mutex guards both fd-1 redirection and [38;5;10mfork[0m.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
I then re-ran negative controls: dropped space in the format string, swapped arguments, and flooring instead of truncating division are all detected. Removing the explicit [38;5;10mflush[0m still passes, which is correct — Rust's [38;5;10mLineWriter[0m flushes on the newline anyway.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[1mOn the undefined inputs:[22m [38;5;10my == 0[0m and [38;5;10mINT_MIN / -1[0m are UB in C and trap with [38;5;10mSIGFPE[0m on x86-64. The Rust code reproduces this with raw [38;5;10midiv[0m rather than Rust's checked semantics, which is the right call — a Rust panic would write to stderr and change observable behavior. I verified the termination signal matches by running each call in a forked child.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
